{% comment %}
  BreadcrumbList structured data.

  WHY THIS SNIPPET EXISTS. The theme already emits Product, Organization and
  WebSite schema, and emits no BreadcrumbList anywhere. That is the one Google
  actually renders in the result itself: without it a product result shows the raw
  URL path, with it the result shows Home > Collection > Product. On a catalogue
  with thousands of SKUs and deep URLs that is the difference between a result
  that reads as a category and one that reads as a slug.

  WHY IT IS BUILT FROM collection.title RATHER THAN THE URL. Shopify serves the
  same product under /products/x and /collections/y/products/x, and only the
  second knows which collection the customer came through. `collection` is set on
  that route and nil on the bare product URL, so the middle crumb appears exactly
  when it is true and is omitted otherwise. Inventing a category from the handle
  would be structured data that contradicts the page, which is worse than none.

  ABSOLUTE URLS, and that is not a style choice: Google requires an absolute URI
  in an @id, and a relative one is silently dropped. `request.origin` gives the
  canonical host including the scheme, so this stays correct on a preview domain,
  a custom domain, and a myshopify domain without a hardcoded string to rot.

  ESCAPING. Every interpolated value goes through `| json`, which quotes AND
  escapes. A product title containing a double quote, which in a technical
  catalogue is routine because inches are written 2", would otherwise produce
  invalid JSON and take the whole block down with it.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign crumb_position = 0
-%}
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      "position": 1,
      {%- comment -%}
        The first crumb is deliberately shop.name and NOT a translation key.

        `theme check` caught the first version of this line: it used
        'general.breadcrumbs.home' | t | default: 'Home', and that key does not
        exist in this theme's locales. The `default` filter does NOT save it,
        because a missing translation renders the literal string
        "Translation missing: en.general.breadcrumbs.home", which is not blank, so
        `default` never fires and Google would have been served that text as the
        breadcrumb name on every page.

        shop.name needs no locale file, is already the brand as the merchant typed
        it, and matches the Organization node the theme emits, so the two agree
        instead of one saying "Home" and the other the company name.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "name": {{ shop.name | json }},
      "item": {{ request.origin | append: routes.root_url | json }}
    }
    {%- if template.name == 'product' -%}
      {%- if collection and collection.title != blank -%}
        ,{
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 2,
          "name": {{ collection.title | json }},
          "item": {{ request.origin | append: collection.url | json }}
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 3,
          "name": {{ product.title | json }},
          "item": {{ request.origin | append: product.url | json }}
        }
      {%- else -%}
        ,{
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 2,
          "name": {{ product.title | json }},
          "item": {{ request.origin | append: product.url | json }}
        }
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- elsif template.name == 'collection' and collection.title != blank -%}
      ,{
        "@type": "ListItem",
        "position": 2,
        "name": {{ collection.title | json }},
        "item": {{ request.origin | append: collection.url | json }}
      }
    {%- elsif template.name == 'article' and article -%}
      ,{
        "@type": "ListItem",
        "position": 2,
        "name": {{ blog.title | json }},
        "item": {{ request.origin | append: blog.url | json }}
      },
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        "position": 3,
        "name": {{ article.title | json }},
        "item": {{ request.origin | append: article.url | json }}
      }
    {%- elsif template.name == 'page' and page.title != blank -%}
      ,{
        "@type": "ListItem",
        "position": 2,
        "name": {{ page.title | json }},
        "item": {{ request.origin | append: page.url | json }}
      }
    {%- endif -%}
  ]
}
</script>
